WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.963
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4407.

2
00:00:00.963 --> 00:00:01.926
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:01.926 --> 00:00:03.852
<v ELIZABETH GELVIN>Alright, today is January 24, 2015.

4
00:00:03.852 --> 00:00:09.951
I am Elizabeth Gelvin, representing the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU [Louisiana State University] Libraries.

5
00:00:09.951 --> 00:00:16.049
We're working in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum and Bill Shearman to document the history of Mossville, Louisiana.

6
00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:22.790
To kick that off, we are holding Mossville Oral History Day, here at the Rigmaiden Recreational Center on Old Spanish Trail.

7
00:00:22.790 --> 00:00:25.358
So, I'm here today with Mrs. Janie Gardner.

8
00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:26.000
<v JANIE GARDNER>Yes.

9
00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.377
<v GELVIN>Thank you for agreeing to be interviewed.

10
00:00:28.377 --> 00:00:31.774
This is going to be short . . . Maybe ten, fifteen minutes.

11
00:00:31.774 --> 00:00:38.226
But we'll be in touch with you later if you're interested in setting up a longer, more extended interview.

12
00:00:38.226 --> 00:00:38.566
<v GARDNER>Okay.

13
00:00:38.566 --> 00:00:38.906
<v GELVIN>Okay?

14
00:00:38.906 --> 00:00:39.245
Alright.

15
00:00:39.245 --> 00:00:44.000
So, to start off, could you please state your full name for me?

16
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
<v GARDNER>My name is Janie Lyons Dotson Gardner.

17
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:48.286
<v GELVIN>Lyons Dotson Gardner?

18
00:00:48.286 --> 00:00:48.714
Wow.

19
00:00:48.714 --> 00:00:49.143
Awesome.

20
00:00:49.143 --> 00:00:52.143
What is your . . . What is your birthday?

21
00:00:52.143 --> 00:00:53.857
When were you born?

22
00:00:53.857 --> 00:00:55.143
<v GARDNER>August 14, 1946.

23
00:00:55.143 --> 00:00:56.000
<v GELVIN>Nineteen forty-six.

24
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:56.857
Okay, great.

25
00:00:56.857 --> 00:00:59.857
And you were born here in Mossville?

26
00:00:59.857 --> 00:01:00.714
<v GARDNER>Lake Charles.

27
00:01:00.714 --> 00:01:02.000
<v GELVIN>In Lake Charles?

28
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:04.571
When did you move to Mossville?

29
00:01:04.571 --> 00:01:06.286
<v GARDNER>My parents adopted me.

30
00:01:06.286 --> 00:01:06.714
<v GELVIN>Okay.

31
00:01:06.714 --> 00:01:09.714
<v GARDNER>So I moved probably shortly after birth.

32
00:01:09.714 --> 00:01:11.000
<v GELVIN>Shortly after birth.

33
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:14.000
And your parents were from Mossville?

34
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:20.430
<v GARDNER>They were . . . My father was originally from Westlake [Louisiana], my mother was originally from Okmulgee, Oklahoma.

35
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:23.645
They moved to Mossville, I'd say, in 1945.

36
00:01:23.645 --> 00:01:24.449
<v GELVIN>Nineteen forty-five.

37
00:01:24.449 --> 00:01:26.458
What are your parents' names?

38
00:01:26.458 --> 00:01:28.467
<v GARDNER>Dave Lyons and Lula Lyons.

39
00:01:28.467 --> 00:01:28.869
<v GELVIN>Awesome.

40
00:01:28.869 --> 00:01:30.879
What were your grandparents' names?

41
00:01:30.879 --> 00:01:36.907
<v GARDNER>My father's father was named Simon or Simeon Lyons, and my grandmother was Mary Lyons.

42
00:01:36.907 --> 00:01:38.916
I don't know my mom's.

43
00:01:38.916 --> 00:01:41.327
<v GELVIN>You don't know your mom's side.

44
00:01:41.327 --> 00:01:44.140
<v GARDNER>No. I don't know everything about her.

45
00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:51.374
I know she had three sisters and three brothers, and my father had two brothers and one sister.

46
00:01:51.374 --> 00:01:53.383
<v GELVIN>Two brothers and one sister.

47
00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:57.000
What did your parents do for a living?

48
00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.729
<v GARDNER>My father worked on refrigeration.

49
00:01:58.729 --> 00:02:04.262
And he . . . And my mother, she was a day worker for a part-time, for a while.

50
00:02:04.262 --> 00:02:07.028
And then from there, she worked what, five

51
00:02:07.028 --> 00:02:09.794
<c.p>days a week for five dollars a day.

52
00:02:09.794 --> 00:02:14.981
And from there she went to Chateau Charles as a . . . Working as a salad girl.

53
00:02:14.981 --> 00:02:19.131
Then she did daycare, well, she did nursery, she was a nanny.

54
00:02:19.131 --> 00:02:21.897
<v GELVIN>Could you explain a salad girl to me?

55
00:02:21.897 --> 00:02:24.664
<v GARDNER>She just made the salads at the restaurant--

56
00:02:24.664 --> 00:02:26.047
<v GELVIN>Just made the salads!

57
00:02:26.047 --> 00:02:26.393
Okay.

58
00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:28.467
Gotcha. [Gardner laughs] I thought so!

59
00:02:28.467 --> 00:02:34.000
But I was just like, I don't know, maybe she's doing something crazy involving salads.

60
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:42.138
<v GARDNER>And then she worked at, I think it was First Baptist Church of Westlake, and she took care of the nursery.

61
00:02:42.138 --> 00:02:42.912
<v GELVIN>Okay, awesome.

62
00:02:42.912 --> 00:02:45.238
How many siblings do you have?

63
00:02:45.238 --> 00:02:45.625
<v GARDNER>One.

64
00:02:45.625 --> 00:02:46.012
<v GELVIN>One?

65
00:02:46.012 --> 00:02:46.400
Awesome.

66
00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:51.050
<v GARDNER>One . . . Well, I have a total of two sisters and a brother,

67
00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:55.700
but with my mom and dad, I had a brother and myself.

68
00:02:55.700 --> 00:02:56.088
<v GELVIN>Awesome.

69
00:02:56.088 --> 00:03:01.900
So, could you maybe share some memories of growing up with your siblings in Mossville?

70
00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:05.000
Like what y'all would do for fun?

71
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:05.286
<v GARDNER>Crawfish.

72
00:03:05.286 --> 00:03:05.571
<v GELVIN>Crawfish?

73
00:03:05.571 --> 00:03:06.714
<v GARDNER>Right across the street.

74
00:03:06.714 --> 00:03:08.143
I lived across the street--

75
00:03:08.143 --> 00:03:09.000
<v GELVIN>Oh, wow.

76
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.290
<v GARDNER>Directly across the street.

77
00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:13.836
We would go in the little ditch, and we would crawfish.

78
00:03:13.836 --> 00:03:15.126
We would climb trees.

79
00:03:15.126 --> 00:03:16.415
I was a tomboy.

80
00:03:16.415 --> 00:03:17.383
I shot marbles.

81
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:20.284
For my twelfth birthday, I got a BB gun.

82
00:03:20.284 --> 00:03:23.831
<v GELVIN>Aw, yeah. [Gardner and Gelvin laughing] That's what I'm talking about.

83
00:03:23.831 --> 00:03:26.087
<v GARDNER>We . . . I had a good life here.

84
00:03:26.087 --> 00:03:27.699
The recreation center was here.

85
00:03:27.699 --> 00:03:34.470
The pool was there, so I went swimming practically every day . . . Never learned to swim, but I went to the pool.

86
00:03:34.470 --> 00:03:39.306
We came across here. [cell phone rings] This was before this time, before this facility.

87
00:03:39.306 --> 00:03:41.240
It was where the school was.

88
00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:47.044
The school was a two-room school, and there was a partition in the middle, so we played there.

89
00:03:47.044 --> 00:03:50.913
And you walked back and forth, down the street with your friends.

90
00:03:50.913 --> 00:03:53.814
So, it was a good place to grow up.

91
00:03:53.814 --> 00:03:55.104
<v GELVIN>Yes, it sounds good.

92
00:03:55.104 --> 00:03:57.683
<v GARDNER>It was a great place to grow up.

93
00:03:57.683 --> 00:04:02.842
You know, it was a place where you didn't realize that you were poor. [Gardner laughs]

94
00:04:02.842 --> 00:04:07.355
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR.>I didn't realize the recreation center was here, and the pool was still here.

95
00:04:07.355 --> 00:04:08.000
<v GARDNER>Yes.

96
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:11.313
<v GELVIN>Would you mind stating your full name for me as well?

97
00:04:11.313 --> 00:04:13.723
Now that I have you on the audio--

98
00:04:13.723 --> 00:04:17.036
<v DOTSON, JR>I am Donald Dotson, Jr. I am her son, her oldest.

99
00:04:17.036 --> 00:04:17.337
<v GELVIN>Awesome.

100
00:04:17.337 --> 00:04:17.639
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR>Yes.

101
00:04:17.639 --> 00:04:20.349
<v GELVIN>Do you have, maybe, some similar memories from childhood?

102
00:04:20.349 --> 00:04:23.964
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR>The same things that she's saying! [You can't believe it?] [all laugh]

103
00:04:23.964 --> 00:04:26.072
<v DAREN DOTSON, SR.>[Daren is Gardner’s youngest son] Exact same!

104
00:04:26.072 --> 00:04:28.482
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR>I didn't realize that they had this facility--

105
00:04:28.482 --> 00:04:29.988
<v GARDNER>When I was growing up--

106
00:04:29.988 --> 00:04:32.096
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR>I didn't realize the history of that.

107
00:04:32.096 --> 00:04:33.000
<v GELVIN>That's awesome.

108
00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.864
<v GARDNER>Look my mom spoiled my sons.

109
00:04:34.864 --> 00:04:39.524
To the point that, one summer they said they wanted a horse and a dog.

110
00:04:39.524 --> 00:04:46.049
So, when they came home for the summer, there was a horse in the backyard and a dog in the backyard.

111
00:04:46.049 --> 00:04:48.845
<v GELVIN>I heard-- [All laugh] I heard that apocryphal story!

112
00:04:48.845 --> 00:04:55.369
<v GARDNER>Before they left, because we lived in Maryland . . . Before they left, my children were running behind some kids with a bike.

113
00:04:55.369 --> 00:04:58.786
My mom went out and bought the bike the same day!

114
00:04:58.786 --> 00:05:01.893
They left two days later, but they had a bike.

115
00:05:01.893 --> 00:05:02.515
<v GELVIN>Aw, man.

116
00:05:02.515 --> 00:05:03.757
<v GARDNER>They were her hearts.

117
00:05:03.757 --> 00:05:05.000
<v GELVIN>Classic grandma move.

118
00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:05.343
<v GARDNER>Yes!

119
00:05:05.343 --> 00:05:07.748
I don't move quite like that. [laughs]

120
00:05:07.748 --> 00:05:09.465
<v NICHELLE LOWE-DOTSON>[Daren Dotson Sr.’s wife] Close.

121
00:05:09.465 --> 00:05:11.526
<v GELVIN>Did you go to school here?

122
00:05:11.526 --> 00:05:11.870
<v GARDNER>Yes.

123
00:05:11.870 --> 00:05:16.335
I graduated . . . I went to school here, in this little area right here.

124
00:05:16.335 --> 00:05:19.426
There was a building . . . Now they call them sharpshooters.

125
00:05:19.426 --> 00:05:25.265
There was a . . . That's where the seventh and eighth graders, six seven and eight, went to school.

126
00:05:25.265 --> 00:05:31.104
And then the room . . . The building that I went had first second and third in one section.

127
00:05:31.104 --> 00:05:35.570
Then there was a partition, and there was fourth and fifth and sixth.

128
00:05:35.570 --> 00:05:38.661
Then there was another sharpshooter where you had lunch.

129
00:05:38.661 --> 00:05:39.004
<v GELVIN>Awesome.

130
00:05:39.004 --> 00:05:40.378
That was right here?

131
00:05:40.378 --> 00:05:42.096
Where the recreational center is?

132
00:05:42.096 --> 00:05:42.439
<v GARDNER>Yes.

133
00:05:42.439 --> 00:05:43.813
Right in this location.

134
00:05:43.813 --> 00:05:52.743
They had an outdoor bathroom, I remember that. [all laugh] Because I used to want to go to the bathroom, and the kids treated me mean!

135
00:05:52.743 --> 00:05:54.117
They wouldn't let me!

136
00:05:54.117 --> 00:05:57.896
Then there was this water trough where you got water outside.

137
00:05:57.896 --> 00:06:02.361
It was this big trough, and they had all these little water spigots.

138
00:06:02.361 --> 00:06:09.230
I went there until I was in the fourth grade, and in the fifth grade they built a new school.

139
00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:10.948
I went to school there.

140
00:06:10.948 --> 00:06:18.504
Now, I only went there until I was in the sixth grade, and my parents moved me . . . Sent me to boarding school.

141
00:06:18.504 --> 00:06:21.596
So, I went to boarding school for four years.

142
00:06:21.596 --> 00:06:24.000
<v GELVIN>Where'd you go to boarding school?

143
00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.091
<v GARDNER>In Lafayette [Louisiana].

144
00:06:25.091 --> 00:06:27.273
It was called Holy Rosary Institute.

145
00:06:27.273 --> 00:06:34.182
I went to school there for four years, and my dad lost his leg . . . He was unable to work.

146
00:06:34.182 --> 00:06:38.909
So, I came home, and I went my last two years at Mossville.

147
00:06:38.909 --> 00:06:40.000
<v GELVIN>At Mossville.

148
00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.025
<v GARDNER>And it was good, because it was a totally different environment, you know?

149
00:06:44.025 --> 00:06:50.838
You had football games, you had all of this freedom that you didn't have in a Catholic school. [Gardner and Gelvin laugh]

150
00:06:50.838 --> 00:06:51.766
<v GELVIN>No nuns, anyway.

151
00:06:51.766 --> 00:06:53.005
<v GARDNER>And it was great.

152
00:06:53.005 --> 00:06:53.934
<v GELVIN>Awesome, that's great.

153
00:06:53.934 --> 00:06:55.173
Do you have any . . .

154
00:06:55.173 --> 00:07:01.365
<v GARDNER>They used to give you . . . They used to have movies, down at this school during the summer, for the kids.

155
00:07:01.365 --> 00:07:04.772
You'd go to movies, I think it was every Tuesday night.

156
00:07:04.772 --> 00:07:10.964
So you could go down to the movie, because there wasn't a whole lot of things going on around here.

157
00:07:10.964 --> 00:07:14.371
But the one thing that my mom taught me . . . You know,

158
00:07:14.371 --> 00:07:17.467
she exposed me to . . . I took ballet, I took tap.

159
00:07:17.467 --> 00:07:19.325
She exposed me to the arts.

160
00:07:19.325 --> 00:07:22.731
Even though she only made twenty five dollars a week, but

161
00:07:22.731 --> 00:07:25.208
<c.p>she still exposed me to all of those.

162
00:07:25.208 --> 00:07:27.995
She brought me up to be a strong woman.

163
00:07:27.995 --> 00:07:32.949
She told me that I was as good as everybody else, no matter who they were.

164
00:07:32.949 --> 00:07:41.000
She taught me not to say yes ma'am and no ma'am, just to say yes and no. It helped me to become a strong individual.

165
00:07:41.000 --> 00:07:46.059
I moved to Maryland . . . Well, I got divorced, got married, moved to Maryland.

166
00:07:46.059 --> 00:07:53.064
But, with only a high school education I was able to still do what I needed to do.

167
00:07:53.064 --> 00:07:56.956
I worked hard, I got . . . I bought my own home.

168
00:07:56.956 --> 00:07:59.291
I took care of my children.

169
00:07:59.291 --> 00:08:07.852
Maybe I never made fifty thousand a year, but I made forty eight! [laughs] But that's with just a high school education.

170
00:08:07.852 --> 00:08:12.133
That didn't give you accounting, that didn't teach you foreign languages.

171
00:08:12.133 --> 00:08:18.360
But she taught me to be a strong individual, to work hard for whatever I wanted.

172
00:08:18.360 --> 00:08:20.695
I was able to do it.

173
00:08:20.695 --> 00:08:25.365
Now I have a son with a master . . . He's a master plumber.

174
00:08:25.365 --> 00:08:27.310
He's getting a journeyman's plumber.

175
00:08:27.310 --> 00:08:34.704
I have a grandson who graduated from Louisiana Tech, and has a bachelor of science and majored in physics.

176
00:08:34.704 --> 00:08:38.985
I have a granddaughter who is currently at McNeese taking nursing.

177
00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:41.320
My daughter-in-law here is in school.

178
00:08:41.320 --> 00:08:43.655
My other daughter-in-law is an RN.

179
00:08:43.655 --> 00:08:45.601
You know, God just blessed.

180
00:08:45.601 --> 00:08:51.049
So from that humble beginning, God just manifested . . . And if I hadn't been here,

181
00:08:51.049 --> 00:08:57.276
hadn't learned the things that I learned here, I wouldn't be the person I am today.

182
00:08:57.276 --> 00:08:57.665
<v GELVIN>Definitely.

183
00:08:57.665 --> 00:09:00.000
<v BILL SHEARMAN>What happened to the property?

184
00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.183
<v GARDNER>We sold it to Sasol just recently.

185
00:09:02.183 --> 00:09:02.806
<v SHEARMAN>Just recently?

186
00:09:02.806 --> 00:09:03.118
<v GARDNER>Yes.

187
00:09:03.118 --> 00:09:04.677
<v UNNAMED>It hasn't been a year.

188
00:09:04.677 --> 00:09:06.548
March . . . March will be a year.

189
00:09:06.548 --> 00:09:06.860
<v GARDNER>Yes.

190
00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:07.172
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

191
00:09:07.172 --> 00:09:09.355
<v GARDNER>So, it was directly across the street.

192
00:09:09.355 --> 00:09:15.591
I grew up across the street from here. [door opens] The house that I originally moved to is still standing.

193
00:09:15.591 --> 00:09:19.022
We lived . . . My father bought the house that is behind there.

194
00:09:19.022 --> 00:09:20.892
Then, eventually, he bought more property.

195
00:09:20.892 --> 00:09:24.323
He had what was called the Lyon's subdivision for a while.

196
00:09:24.323 --> 00:09:26.194
Then he sold the land off.

197
00:09:26.194 --> 00:09:29.000
<v SHEARMAN>How long have you been back in Mossville?

198
00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:34.114
<v GARDNER>I've been here . . . I moved back from Maryland about two and a half years ago.

199
00:09:34.114 --> 00:09:34.455
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

200
00:09:34.455 --> 00:09:37.864
<v GARDNER>You know, I never thought I'd come back to Louisiana.

201
00:09:37.864 --> 00:09:39.227
But I'm happy here.

202
00:09:39.227 --> 00:09:40.591
I like it here.

203
00:09:40.591 --> 00:09:41.955
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR>I brought you here.

204
00:09:41.955 --> 00:09:45.705
<v GARDNER>Yes, he brought me here! [all laugh] And I'm happy here.

205
00:09:45.705 --> 00:09:51.500
Now that it's all gone, you can look back and see how great it was, being here.

206
00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:54.227
<v SHEARMAN>You family retained property for all these years?

207
00:09:54.227 --> 00:09:54.568
<v GARDNER>Yes.

208
00:09:54.568 --> 00:09:55.591
<v SHEARMAN>Good. [phone rings]

209
00:09:55.591 --> 00:09:55.932
<v GARDNER>Yes.

210
00:09:55.932 --> 00:09:57.977
It wasn't until they bought it.

211
00:09:57.977 --> 00:10:02.409
Because he [gesturing to son] lived in my house here until . . . until March.

212
00:10:02.409 --> 00:10:03.432
We were here.

213
00:10:03.432 --> 00:10:04.795
We weren't going anywhere.

214
00:10:04.795 --> 00:10:10.591
But, you know, if the plant's going to take over everything . . . Yeah, I don't care to be . . .

215
00:10:10.591 --> 00:10:14.000
<v NICHELLE LOWE-DOTSON>[Dotson Sr.’s wife speaks] [. . . ?] exposed to all those chemicals.

216
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.481
<v GARDNER>Believe it or not, my mom lived to be ninety two.

217
00:10:18.481 --> 00:10:18.889
<v SHEARMAN>Good.

218
00:10:18.889 --> 00:10:21.741
<v GARDNER>My dad lived to be seventy nine.

219
00:10:21.741 --> 00:10:23.778
But my dad had diabetes.

220
00:10:23.778 --> 00:10:25.000
<v SHEARMAN>Yes ma'am.

221
00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.160
<v GARDNER>He had lost both of his legs.

222
00:10:27.160 --> 00:10:30.247
But he was the type of person who never stopped.

223
00:10:30.247 --> 00:10:31.481
He was never handicapped.

224
00:10:31.481 --> 00:10:33.951
My daddy built a boat from his wheelchair.

225
00:10:33.951 --> 00:10:37.654
I mean, a boat that you could put out in the water!

226
00:10:37.654 --> 00:10:41.975
The day he died, he was building a rack to put his motor on.

227
00:10:41.975 --> 00:10:47.222
I mean, that man, he would get in there, wash clothes, mop the floors, wash the cars.

228
00:10:47.222 --> 00:10:50.000
Daddy would do anything he wanted to do.

229
00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:52.449
<v DAREN DOTSON SR.>He didn't let that wheelchair slow him down.

230
00:10:52.449 --> 00:10:59.184
<v GARDNER>No. So, that gave you the inspiration to just keep going because, hey if they could do this, we could do it.

231
00:10:59.184 --> 00:11:01.633
She had her ninetieth birthday party right here.

232
00:11:01.633 --> 00:11:03.163
<v GELVIN>Here at the rec center?

233
00:11:03.163 --> 00:11:03.469
<v DAREN DOTSON SR>Yes.

234
00:11:03.469 --> 00:11:04.694
Oh and could dance!

235
00:11:04.694 --> 00:11:07.143
At ninety years old, could two-step. [Gelvin laughs]

236
00:11:07.143 --> 00:11:09.592
<v GARDNER>Oh, I was laughing, I made people laugh.

237
00:11:09.592 --> 00:11:12.347
This is a joke about Mom, but it's true.

238
00:11:12.347 --> 00:11:17.551
My mom told me when she was seventy six, she said, "I don't understand you young people.

239
00:11:17.551 --> 00:11:20.000
You're always saying you can't find a man."

240
00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:26.122
She said, "Here I am, cancer, with a pacemaker, and I just found me one a year younger than me!"

241
00:11:26.122 --> 00:11:32.857
She married him . . . She divorced him two years later because she said because she didn't need that hassle! [all laugh] You never

242
00:11:32.857 --> 00:11:34.694
<c.p>saw her without her makeup on.

243
00:11:34.694 --> 00:11:38.980
They came to visit her one day, and she didn't have her makeup on.

244
00:11:38.980 --> 00:11:41.122
She shut the door in their face.

245
00:11:41.122 --> 00:11:47.857
Then she went in, put her makeup on, put her stockings on, got dressed, and then invited them all in. [all laugh]

246
00:11:47.857 --> 00:11:48.776
<v GELVIN>A true lady.

247
00:11:48.776 --> 00:11:50.000
<v SHEARMAN>First things first.

248
00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:51.604
<v GARDNER>She was an awesome woman.

249
00:11:51.604 --> 00:11:52.246
<v DOTSON>[. . .?] a lady.

250
00:11:52.246 --> 00:11:55.776
<v GARDNER>I didn't take after her. [laughs] I had a good life.

251
00:11:55.776 --> 00:11:56.738
I really did.

252
00:11:56.738 --> 00:11:59.947
To say that I was able to have that life,

253
00:11:59.947 --> 00:12:05.402
that meant that they struggled to send me to school so that I'd have a better life.

254
00:12:05.402 --> 00:12:10.215
<v DAREN DOTSON SR>You know, I would have never thought what you told me . . . Five dollars a day?

255
00:12:10.215 --> 00:12:17.595
The way they lived, I would have never thought they was like that. [computer noise goes off several times in the background] Because,

256
00:12:17.595 --> 00:12:19.520
to me, they always had money.

257
00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:21.125
I would have never know.

258
00:12:21.125 --> 00:12:22.729
That just goes to show . . .

259
00:12:22.729 --> 00:12:24.333
<v GARDNER>The freezer was always full.

260
00:12:24.333 --> 00:12:30.751
There was always a side of beef in the freezer, there was always a half a pig in the freezer.

261
00:12:30.751 --> 00:12:32.034
There was always food.

262
00:12:32.034 --> 00:12:33.639
No, I didn't live in

263
00:12:33.639 --> 00:12:36.526
<c.p>the fancy house like, maybe a couple people did.

264
00:12:36.526 --> 00:12:39.735
But my house was a house where everybody was welcome.

265
00:12:39.735 --> 00:12:41.660
So people came, and we enjoyed.

266
00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:48.078
As a kid, I didn't realize we used to go take the steaks out of the freezer and cook them.

267
00:12:48.078 --> 00:12:55.458
Now, shoot, if someone look like that wanted to take my steak, I'd hurt them! [all laugh] But, you know, they didn't care.

268
00:12:55.458 --> 00:12:58.667
When they got me, they were already in their forties.

269
00:12:58.667 --> 00:13:02.838
My mom must have been forty, my dad must have been forty eight.

270
00:13:02.838 --> 00:13:06.688
The one thing she didn't do . . . She didn't teach me to cook.

271
00:13:06.688 --> 00:13:08.614
She didn't teach me to clean.

272
00:13:08.614 --> 00:13:15.994
She didn't teach me all the things I was going to need to know as I grew up, because they just spoiled us.

273
00:13:15.994 --> 00:13:18.561
They spoiled me rotten, they spoiled them rotten.

274
00:13:18.561 --> 00:13:19.844
They were good people.

275
00:13:19.844 --> 00:13:21.769
And this was a good community.

276
00:13:21.769 --> 00:13:24.336
You could walk down the street at night.

277
00:13:24.336 --> 00:13:33.000
I'd have to walk my friend to her house, and then she'd walk me to my house, and then I'd walk her back to her house.

278
00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.160
One funny episode, we ran into a German shepherd.

279
00:13:36.160 --> 00:13:38.618
I'd never seen a German shepherd before.

280
00:13:38.618 --> 00:13:44.937
So, I'm telling my girlfriend . . . Her name is Frank . . . I said, "Don't run because it will chase you."

281
00:13:44.937 --> 00:13:51.608
By the time I said, "Don't run," I looked up, she was already home! [all laugh] I'm standing there.

282
00:13:51.608 --> 00:13:53.715
So I took off running, too!

283
00:13:53.715 --> 00:13:56.524
It was a good place to grow up.

284
00:13:56.524 --> 00:13:58.279
The kids are good people.

285
00:13:58.279 --> 00:14:05.301
You didn't hear about all of this killing, drugs . . . You know, none of that stuff when I was growing up.

286
00:14:05.301 --> 00:14:11.270
I don't know what goes on now, but you didn't have that when I was growing up.

287
00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:14.078
It was a good place to grow up.

288
00:14:14.078 --> 00:14:15.483
The teachers were good.

289
00:14:15.483 --> 00:14:16.887
They cared about you.

290
00:14:16.887 --> 00:14:17.940
They encouraged you.

291
00:14:17.940 --> 00:14:20.047
I did, I went to work.

292
00:14:20.047 --> 00:14:23.909
Like I said, my mom worked in day . . . Did day labor.

293
00:14:23.909 --> 00:14:26.016
See, I went to work once.

294
00:14:26.016 --> 00:14:27.771
I did, no, two days.

295
00:14:27.771 --> 00:14:30.229
I said, "This is not for me."

296
00:14:30.229 --> 00:14:33.389
I can't be cleaning nobody's house for five dollars.

297
00:14:33.389 --> 00:14:35.144
That was not for me.

298
00:14:35.144 --> 00:14:37.953
But, had I not gone out . . . Found out.

299
00:14:37.953 --> 00:14:40.060
Then I realized I wanted more.

300
00:14:40.060 --> 00:14:41.815
So, I went to school.

301
00:14:41.815 --> 00:14:46.028
I went to [?], but then I moved to Maryland, so I didn't.

302
00:14:46.028 --> 00:14:46.730
I did

303
00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:48.135
<c.p>take courses at [PG?].

304
00:14:48.135 --> 00:14:55.157
But if you study hard, and you put your best foot forward, you can be anything you want to be . . .

305
00:14:55.157 --> 00:15:00.774
<v SHEARMAN>Ma'am, does your . . . Do your memories of Mossville go back to when there was no electricity?

306
00:15:00.774 --> 00:15:01.125
<v GARDNER>No!

307
00:15:01.125 --> 00:15:06.743
Look . . . When I came up, I had . . . I just assumed there was lights, there was telephone.

308
00:15:06.743 --> 00:15:10.956
We didn't have a party line . . . Some people had a party line.

309
00:15:10.956 --> 00:15:12.712
I also had a TV.

310
00:15:12.712 --> 00:15:13.063
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

311
00:15:13.063 --> 00:15:17.627
<v GARDNER>So, I always had everything that everybody . . . That I thought everybody else had.

312
00:15:17.627 --> 00:15:25.000
So, no. I didn't even go out . . . My house didn't even have an outside bathroom as far as I know.

313
00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.514
<v SHEARMAN>Was Old Spanish Trail gravel when you were a child?

314
00:15:28.514 --> 00:15:29.919
<v GARDNER>No, it was paved!

315
00:15:29.919 --> 00:15:31.676
It would get really hot.

316
00:15:31.676 --> 00:15:33.432
If you were wearing flip-flops,

317
00:15:33.432 --> 00:15:42.216
your shoes would stay . . . You could be running across and your shoes would stay on the street. [laughs] But they may have just paved it,

318
00:15:42.216 --> 00:15:43.973
you know, around the time.

319
00:15:43.973 --> 00:15:47.486
The road in front of my house is still gravel.

320
00:15:47.486 --> 00:15:50.297
So all of that was still gravel then.

321
00:15:50.297 --> 00:15:51.000
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

322
00:15:51.000 --> 00:15:57.380
<v GARDNER>I am old, but I ain't that old! [all laugh] No, I don't remember it ever being gravel.

323
00:15:57.380 --> 00:15:57.734
<v SHEARMAN>Okay.

324
00:15:57.734 --> 00:15:58.443
Thank you.

325
00:15:58.443 --> 00:16:01.633
<v GELVIN>Well, I have a few more questions for you.

326
00:16:01.633 --> 00:16:01.987
<v GARDNER>Okay.

327
00:16:01.987 --> 00:16:04.823
<v GELVIN>Before we wrap things up, if that's okay.

328
00:16:04.823 --> 00:16:05.177
<v GARDNER>Yes.

329
00:16:05.177 --> 00:16:16.519
<v GELVIN>I wanted to ask you . . . After sharing all these great stories with me about Mossville . . . And just all these stories about Mossville growing up . . . What does the word 'home' mean to you?

330
00:16:16.519 --> 00:16:19.000
What does home mean to you?

331
00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:19.424
<v GARDNER>Here.

332
00:16:19.424 --> 00:16:23.241
<v GELVIN>Could you tell me a little bit about why?

333
00:16:23.241 --> 00:16:25.361
<v GARDNER>This was all I knew.

334
00:16:25.361 --> 00:16:32.147
Even though I spent most of my life . . . I spent more of my life in Maryland.

335
00:16:32.147 --> 00:16:36.387
I thought, when I was in Maryland that was home.

336
00:16:36.387 --> 00:16:42.325
But coming back . . . You realize this is home. [voice cracking with emotion] You know?

337
00:16:42.325 --> 00:16:47.838
Everything about this is home, because this is what I grew up with.

338
00:16:47.838 --> 00:16:52.503
This has helped to mold me to be who I am.

339
00:16:52.503 --> 00:16:55.047
This is where my friends are.

340
00:16:55.047 --> 00:17:00.984
I don't see a lot of them now, because I was gone so long,

341
00:17:00.984 --> 00:17:06.073
but this is . . . When you think back, this is what you remember.

342
00:17:06.073 --> 00:17:07.770
You remember shooting marbles.

343
00:17:07.770 --> 00:17:09.890
You remember climbing the trees.

344
00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.010
You remember riding the bikes.

345
00:17:12.010 --> 00:17:14.555
You remember walking to the store.

346
00:17:14.555 --> 00:17:17.948
We had a little community store called Rougeau’s.

347
00:17:17.948 --> 00:17:23.461
Mr. Rougeau would come and get my dad . . . Every weekend, they went fishing.

348
00:17:23.461 --> 00:17:30.246
So you always had fresh fish . . . Had crabs . . . And all the things, that means a lot.

349
00:17:30.246 --> 00:17:35.335
This was home, because this was where my mom and dad was.

350
00:17:35.335 --> 00:17:40.000
<v GELVIN>And . . . On that note, what does community mean to you?

351
00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:40.361
<v GARDNER>Family.

352
00:17:40.361 --> 00:17:43.969
They say it takes a village to raise a child.

353
00:17:43.969 --> 00:17:47.938
Here, when I was growing up . . . You did wrong in school,

354
00:17:47.938 --> 00:17:56.237
you got in trouble all the way to the house. [Gardner and Gelvin laugh] The person over here heard you got in trouble,

355
00:17:56.237 --> 00:18:00.928
so you might've got a pop there . . . And you might have got here,

356
00:18:00.928 --> 00:18:05.619
and you know you were going to get popped when you got home.

357
00:18:05.619 --> 00:18:07.423
Everybody looked out for everybody.

358
00:18:07.423 --> 00:18:10.309
If you had something, then nobody was hungry.

359
00:18:10.309 --> 00:18:12.474
That's how I see community now.

360
00:18:12.474 --> 00:18:14.639
I see it where I live.

361
00:18:14.639 --> 00:18:17.526
I want to know my people around me.

362
00:18:17.526 --> 00:18:22.216
In Maryland, you had . . . You never knew who lived next door to you.

363
00:18:22.216 --> 00:18:23.660
<v DAREN DOTSON SR>Didn't know your neighbors.

364
00:18:23.660 --> 00:18:28.351
<v GARDNER>You know, you see them, you holler at them, and that was it.

365
00:18:28.351 --> 00:18:30.155
But you knew those people.

366
00:18:30.155 --> 00:18:31.598
You knew the people.

367
00:18:31.598 --> 00:18:34.124
That's what I think community is . . . family.

368
00:18:34.124 --> 00:18:39.897
<v GELVIN>What to you is the most important thing about Mossville that you want people to remember?

369
00:18:39.897 --> 00:18:43.866
That they might not find in history books or news articles?

370
00:18:43.866 --> 00:18:50.000
Like, what's sort of the most important thing about Mossville that you'd like people to know?

371
00:18:50.000 --> 00:18:57.476
<v GARDNER>That it was a great place to grow up in . . . That you did have that love here.

372
00:18:57.476 --> 00:18:59.236
You had that family.

373
00:18:59.236 --> 00:19:01.435
We all loved each other.

374
00:19:01.435 --> 00:19:09.791
Everybody . . . There was nobody, no matter how high you thought you were or how low you thought you were.

375
00:19:09.791 --> 00:19:11.550
Everybody was an individual.

376
00:19:11.550 --> 00:19:18.147
You just . . . [sniffs] You know, the people next door . . . They didn't have a whole lot.

377
00:19:18.147 --> 00:19:20.785
They had a bunch of kids.

378
00:19:20.785 --> 00:19:29.141
They were Braxtons, I think . . . They used the bathroom in the little building . . . But, you know, it didn't matter!

379
00:19:29.141 --> 00:19:30.901
You all played together.

380
00:19:30.901 --> 00:19:33.979
Everybody saw each other as just individuals.

381
00:19:33.979 --> 00:19:36.618
That's what people need to know.

382
00:19:36.618 --> 00:19:39.257
That this was a good community.

383
00:19:39.257 --> 00:19:46.733
The fact that it's not going to be here any longer doesn't mean that it didn't exist.

384
00:19:46.733 --> 00:19:54.649
It existed, and it will still exist . . . And that it was their loss that it's not here anymore.

385
00:19:54.649 --> 00:20:03.005
<v GELVIN>Well, before we wrap up, is there anything you or anyone else would like to add onto this interview?

386
00:20:03.005 --> 00:20:06.963
Anything else y'all would like to say about Mossville?

387
00:20:06.963 --> 00:20:11.801
You'll definitely have another opportunity in the future to . . . If you . . .

388
00:20:11.801 --> 00:20:13.120
<v DONALD DOTSON, JR>Not right now.

389
00:20:13.120 --> 00:20:14.000
<v GARDNER>No.

390
00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:19.724
<v LOWE-DOTSON>The only memory I have here . . . I've only been here three years, in the house that was sold.

391
00:20:19.724 --> 00:20:22.903
<v GELVIN>Would you mind actually saying your full name for me?

392
00:20:22.903 --> 00:20:23.539
<v LOWE-DOTSON>I'm sorry.

393
00:20:23.539 --> 00:20:24.175
Nichelle Lowe-Dotson.

394
00:20:24.175 --> 00:20:27.991
I had my daughter here in Louisiana, Lake Charles . . . At forty six.

395
00:20:27.991 --> 00:20:33.078
She had her first birthday party right across the way here in the little playground area.

396
00:20:33.078 --> 00:20:34.668
You know, it was nice.

397
00:20:34.668 --> 00:20:41.028
First birthday is a big deal, so that's one of the things I'm definitely going to remember about being here.

398
00:20:41.028 --> 00:20:47.387
I met a lot of good people here that his mom introduced me to . . . Or grew up with his mom,

399
00:20:47.387 --> 00:20:51.521
and had stories to tell me about his mom. [laughs] Stuff like that.

400
00:20:51.521 --> 00:20:56.608
So, I've met a lot of good people here, in this area, since I've been here.

401
00:20:56.608 --> 00:21:01.378
I came here from Maryland myself, and I didn't know anyone . . . Was my husband's people.

402
00:21:01.378 --> 00:21:01.696
<v GELVIN>Gotcha.

403
00:21:01.696 --> 00:21:05.194
<v LOWE-DOTSON>So they welcomed me in and made me feel at home.

404
00:21:05.194 --> 00:21:08.691
That's one thing I am definitely going to remember about Mossville.

405
00:21:08.691 --> 00:21:10.917
I hate to see that it's gone.

406
00:21:10.917 --> 00:21:12.507
<v GARDNER>You never meet a stranger.

407
00:21:12.507 --> 00:21:15.051
That's the thing, you don’t meet a stranger.

408
00:21:15.051 --> 00:21:17.276
You know, if you don't know them,

409
00:21:17.276 --> 00:21:22.046
you can still have a conversation . . . They'll still treat you . . . They'll give you a hug.

410
00:21:22.046 --> 00:21:23.000
<v DOTSON JR>Everybody waves.

411
00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:26.720
<v GARDNER>Everybody says hello and waves when they see you.

412
00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:32.507
So, that's what I think the difference in Mossville and living in the city.

413
00:21:32.507 --> 00:21:38.707
I mean, even if it's Louisiana city. . . It's still the city, and it's a difference.

414
00:21:38.707 --> 00:21:39.120
<v GELVIN>Gotcha.

415
00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:45.733
Yes, well, I want to thank you on behalf of the project, but also just personally.

416
00:21:45.733 --> 00:21:49.453
Thank you for sharing your story with us today.

417
00:21:49.453 --> 00:21:50.280
<v GARDNER>Thank you.

418
00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:51.107
<v GELVIN>Thank you.

419
00:21:51.107 --> 00:21:52.347
<v DAREN DOTSON SR>It was awesome.

420
00:21:52.347 --> 00:21:54.000
<v GELVIN>Thanks, you guys.

421
00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:54.200
<c.p>[End Tape 4407.

422
00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:54.200
End Session I.]
